# See the directory map in lunar-oracle-gameplan.md. This file enforces it.
# If this file and that table ever disagree, the table is the statement of intent and this
# file is the bug. Do not resolve a disagreement by editing the table.
#
# Two properties every rule below is subordinate to:
#   1. ANCHORED. Every path rule begins with `/`, so it names one place in this repository
#      and cannot be re-matched at depth by a folder the merge taxonomy invents later.
#      The only deliberately unanchored rule is the litter rule at the bottom.
#   2. FAILS CLOSED under literature/. A file type nobody has thought of yet is excluded
#      until somebody deliberately admits it.
#
# AMENDED AT 2.14 (The Systems Engineer). Property 1 now has TWO deliberate exceptions, not
# one, and the second is the section immediately below. A published-source carrier must be
# excluded WHEREVER it appears, so anchoring it would defeat it: the measured hole was that
# x.pdf, docs/x.pdf, oracle/x.pdf and tools/x.pdf all committed cleanly while literature/x.pdf
# did not. Property 1's reason -- "cannot be re-matched at depth by a folder the merge taxonomy
# invents later" -- is exactly the behaviour wanted here and exactly why it is inverted. The
# exception is stated rather than taken, because a rule that quietly breaks the file's own
# declared invariant is how the invariant stops being checked.

# --- Published-source carriers, repository-wide (loose end E1; suite rows PDF-2, PDF-3) ----
# DELIBERATELY UNANCHORED. See the amendment note above.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A LICENCE FOOTNOTE. This repository ships summaries the project wrote. It
# does not ship the publications it summarises, and Wave 3 pulls 224,042,382 bytes of PDF into
# literature/_pdf/. That store is ignored by an anchored rule below, and an anchored rule stops
# at its anchor: before this section landed, a PDF dropped anywhere else in the tree committed.
#
# CASE. Written as character classes, not as a pair of *.pdf / *.PDF lines, because a pair
# covers two of the eight case permutations of a three-letter extension and misses x.Pdf. The
# classes are exhaustive by construction and are correct at core.ignorecase=false, which is the
# setting CHK-12 asserts under and the setting a Linux clone actually has.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE CONTAINMENT MECHANISM, and reading it as one is the error the check exists to
# prevent. A path rule matches a NAME. It cannot see source-1.pdf.bak, whose extension is .bak,
# and it cannot see a PDF renamed to x.md. tools/check_no_sources.js (CHK-13) is the content
# half; this is the name half; neither substitutes for the other. See 1.1 section 4.1: the
# enforcement layer fails closed on unknown file TYPES and is blind to unknown CONTENT inside
# an admitted type.
#
# Measured before this section landed: zero source carriers were tracked, so nothing already in
# the repository is orphaned by it. `git ls-files | grep -icE '\.(pdf|djvu|epub|docx?|pptx?|ps|tiff?)$'` = 0.
*.[pP][dD][fF]
*.[dD][jJ][vV][uU]
*.[eE][pP][uU][bB]
*.[dD][oO][cC]
*.[dD][oO][cC][xX]
*.[pP][pP][tT]
*.[pP][pP][tT][xX]
*.[pP][sS]
*.[tT][iI][fF]
*.[tT][iI][fF][fF]

# AMENDED AT 2.20 (The Systems Engineer). The ten classes above stopped at the WORD-PROCESSOR and
# PRESENTATION members of the office family and admitted the SPREADSHEET ones, and .xlsx and .docx
# ARE THE SAME CONTAINER FORMAT -- a zip of XML parts. A boundary that admits one and excludes the
# other is arbitrary rather than principled, and an arbitrary boundary is one nobody can reason
# about at the moment they are deciding whether their file is on the safe side of it.
#
# MEASURED FIRST, and the measurement moved the fix. The Wave 2 open reported that xls, xlsx, zip,
# rar and 7z "all COMMIT". A 76-cell git check-ignore matrix says something narrower and more
# useful: they commit at the REPOSITORY ROOT and under oracle/, tools/ and cr_scratch/, and they
# are already ignored at every depth under /literature/**, /findings/** and /_intake/ by the
# deny-by-default blocks below. So the hole is the WORKING TREE, not the corpus -- which is the
# opposite of where a reader of that sentence would look, and it is the tree a merge staging step
# actually writes into.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY, because the omission is the considered half. zip, rar, 7z, tar, gz
# and tgz are NOT ignored. They are in tools/check_no_sources.js instead, on the .txt precedent
# stated below: an archive has honest non-source uses, and IGNORING an extension with honest uses
# hides the legitimate case SILENTLY, whereas BLOCKING it names the file and a person rules on it
# in one line. CHK-13 sits on pre-commit and blocks, so an archive cannot be committed either way;
# the difference is entirely whether the person finds out. It matters here more than usually:
# 2.11 pulls roughly 224 MB in Wave 3 and the natural shape for that to arrive in is one zip.
*.[xX][lL][sS]
*.[xX][lL][sS][xX]
*.[rR][tT][fF]
*.[oO][dD][tT]
*.[oO][dD][sS]
*.[oO][dD][pP]

# --- Working copies of upstream repositories ------------------------------------------
# Cloned by the bootstrap in CLAUDE.md. Never committed, never vendored: the app in lsei/
# is an authority on the model, and a copy of an authority drifts away from it silently
# while continuing to answer questions. Anchored, so that a corpus taxonomy folder named
# literature/lsei/ is not swallowed by the rule that hides the working copy.
/cr-agents/
/lsei/

# --- Intake staging --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Material on its way into literature/. Not part of the repository, and not the corpus's
# permanent home: permanence attaches to the repository, not to this path. The merge is
# not complete until this directory is empty; the exit criterion is a map row, not a
# comment here. This rule is KEPT after _intake/ is deleted, so that a second corpus
# staged here later fails closed rather than shipping on the next `git add -A`.
/_intake/

# --- Stale bootstrap target -------------------------------------------------------------
# The working copies were flattened from deps/ to the repository root at Setup rev. 1 and
# nothing writes deps/ any more. Before the patterns above were anchored, `cr-agents/`
# matched at any depth and caught a resurrected deps/ by accident. Anchoring removed that
# accident, so the safety net is stated deliberately or it does not exist. This rule stops
# a stale bootstrap's 400 MB of somebody else's repository from being committed; it does
# not detect one. Detection is the bootstrap suite's, at 6.1.
/deps/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# literature/ is DENY BY DEFAULT. Only this project's own .md summaries ship.
#
# The corpus sits beside its source material on the author's disk: PDFs, and at least
# three .txt files carrying full treaty text. Those are published works this project does
# not own. An allow-list that named only *.pdf would have shipped the .txt files on merge
# day, and would have shipped *.PDF on any case-sensitive clone. Deny-by-default fails
# closed instead: a new source file type added six months from now is excluded until
# somebody deliberately admits it.
#
# The second line is not decoration. Without it git never descends into a subdirectory of
# literature/ and the third line never gets the chance to re-admit anything below the top
# level. Verified: removing it makes literature/<topic>/*.md invisible to `git status`.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/literature/**
!/literature/**/
!/literature/**/*.md
# The corpus's own machine-readable files, named as literals. The rule above was written for a
# shelf of summaries and silently excluded every non-.md file the corpus needs; the failure was
# invisible for four sub-steps because the .md count still passes and retrieval degrades quietly
# rather than failing. Literals rather than !*.tsv so that a future .tsv is a decision, not a
# side effect of this one.
!/literature/FIELDS.tsv
!/literature/INDEX.tsv

# --- The source-PDF store (resolves loose end D6) ----------------------------------------
# literature/_pdf/<taxonomy>/ holds source PDFs pulled from outside this repository, filed
# under the same taxonomy names as the summaries but not interleaved with them. Nothing
# here ships, ever, including .md: a summary written into the PDF store is a filing error,
# and re-admitting .md here would ship the filing error. This rule sits AFTER the two
# re-admissions above so that it wins over them; moving it above them silently reverses it.
/literature/_pdf/

# --- Machine-written install state -------------------------------------------------------
# One root-level file, written by the bootstrap, describing THIS install: schema version,
# the ref each working copy was last verified against, whether source PDFs are present
# here, the corpus provenance digest, and the first-run flag. Ignored because a committed
# first-run flag suppresses the opening sequence for every person who clones afterwards,
# which is the exact opposite of what "once" is supposed to mean.
#
# Named as a literal, not a pattern, and that is load-bearing. The rule that keeps this row
# honest is: anything that must survive a clone is not state, it is content, and it belongs
# in the gameplan or in the corpus. Loose end E12's tracked ref record is content. A
# convenience wildcard here — /*state*, /*.lock — would eventually swallow it, and the
# failure would present as "the ref we verified against is missing on a fresh clone."
/.oracle-state.json
# The write-temp, as a second literal rather than a wildcard, for the same reason the line
# above is a literal. It is currently covered only by the unanchored *.tmp litter rule at the
# foot of this file, which is a different rule with a different purpose; a state file that
# survives a clone because someone tightened the litter rule is the failure this row prevents.
/.oracle-state.json.tmp

# --- The findings shelf --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same posture as literature/ above, and for the same reason. The author ruled at the Step 0
# gate that the FA1-FA8 deliverables get their own shelf with its own retrieval contract,
# which makes findings/ a corpus directory rather than an ordinary folder. Deny by default,
# re-admit directories so the walk works, re-admit .md only. A PDF or a .docx dropped here
# ships silently otherwise, and the two shelves would enforce opposite postures on the same
# kind of object.
/findings/**
!/findings/**/
!/findings/**/*.md

# --- Editor and OS litter ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately unanchored: litter appears at any depth and has no home directory.
*.tmp
